  │ 18   │ 16:45–19:50 Azul CGH ·  │ 2h00 │ 19:05–21:50 Azul ·   │ R$     │
  │ jan  │ R$ 1.380                │      │ R$ 1.987             │ 3.367  │
  └──────┴─────────────────────────┴──────┴──────────────────────┴────────┘

  19 Jan wins on every axis: cheapest total, lands 17:35 with the evening free,
  and the price is identical whether you return 26, 27 or 28 Jan — 7, 8 or 9
  nights for the same money.

  Now, the issues your constraints create:

  1. Rio barely flies to Recife at night. Between 19:00 and 22:00 there is
     exactly one GIG–REC flight (Azul 19:05→21:50). So any SP flight arriving
     after 18:00 has only one possible partner — and it's an expensive one (R$
     1.987–2.184 vs ~R$ 1.850 for the afternoon GOL flights). This is why the 18
     Jan option, despite having the cheapest SP ticket in the whole dataset (R$
     1.380), ends up costing the most overall.
  2. The 22:00 cutoff costs you ~R$ 84 — but only on the Rio ticket. The
     cheapest GIG–REC fare anywhere in these searches is GOL 21:20→00:10⁺¹ at R$
     1.740, and it lands after midnight. On the São Paulo side the cutoff costs
     nothing.
  3. 29 of the 33 pairs involve at least one "passagens separadas" fare — two
     separate tickets sold together. If the outbound is delayed, no airline owes
     you a rebooking. Worth knowing since you're already buying two independent
     tickets.
  4. The cheap São Paulo fares are all Congonhas. GRU departures run R$ 150–250
     more for the same time slot.

  The 1–4h window is not your bottleneck — median wait across all 33 options is
  2h00. Destination and the 22:00 cutoff do almost all the filtering.

  One thing I assumed: I read "1–4h earlier" as the gap between the two arrivals
  in Recife, since that's the waiting time at the airport. Say the word if you
  meant departure times instead and I'll re-run it.

